**Lemma 7.2 (the lower chain and strict equality information).** Every
original finite design obeys, for positive diagonal $\tau$,

$$
p_H^{\rm acq}(E\tau E^\dagger)\geq p_H^L(E\tau E^\dagger)
 \geq\operatorname{tr}(G_0(s)\tau).
\tag{CA.38}
$$

Consequently its original four-sign mean at height $y$ is at least
$g_y(s)$. If one such original mean is equal, the actual aggregate high
coefficient matrix $H=H_++H_-$ has diagonal $(p,q,0)$, and the supported
compression of $G_H^{\rm acq}-G_H^L$ is zero.

Proof. We recall the credited lower proof to identify exactly its equality
requirements. Analytically average the actual coefficient pairs under
$U_{e,n}$, acting by $C_{e,n}=\operatorname{diag}(1,en,e)$ and relabelling
$r$ to $enr$, and then average with complex conjugation. These operations
preserve positivity, the full singular cone, coefficient sum and binary
constraints. Indeed $\Theta(CZC)=U\Theta(Z)U^\dagger$, and the signed
record effect transforms to the same required one. Conjugation also
preserves the real polynomial locus and real prescribed effects.
Diagonal costs and the diagonal of aggregate $H$ are unchanged. This is
an argument about matrices and inequalities, not physical sign gates or
a conjugation channel.

The averaged total live matrices and their high parts have the forms

$$
Z_r^0=\frac12\begin{pmatrix}1&rs&0\\rs&1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
H_r^{\rm av}=\frac12\begin{pmatrix}P_0&rt_0&0\\rt_0&Q_0&0\\0&0&Z_0\end{pmatrix}.
\tag{CA.39}
$$

The remaining sign at fixed $en$ kills $DS,TS$ entries; opposite records
have equal diagonals, hence $1/2$ in their total matrices. The binary
$01$ effect fixes their real $DT$ entry because
$\operatorname{Re}\Theta(Z)_{01}=\kappa\operatorname{Re}Z_{DT}$, and
failure effects kill coordinate $0$. Positivity of high and low gives

$$
s\leq F(P_0,Q_0):=\sqrt{P_0Q_0}+\sqrt{(1-P_0)(1-Q_0)},\qquad
P_0\geq k+\alpha Q_0+\beta Z_0.
\tag{CA.40}
$$

The second inequality is the sum of
$|u|^2\leq\alpha|v|^2+\beta|w|^2$ over the actual low products.
It is only a necessary budget here; no trace sufficiency is assumed.
For $A=\operatorname{tr}(D^\dagger D\tau)$,
$B=\operatorname{tr}(T^\dagger T\tau)$,
$C=\operatorname{tr}(S^\dagger S\tau)$, the unchanged high live cost is
$P_0A+Q_0B+Z_0C$. In the weak regime (CA.40) bounds it below by $kA$.

For the strong regime $F(k+\alpha q,q)$ strictly increases from
$\sqrt\ell$ to $1$ on $[0,q_{\max}]$. To verify this, the angular gap
$\arcsin\sqrt{k+\alpha q}-\arcsin\sqrt q$ is positive in the interior
and strictly decreases: its derivative sign is determined by

$$
(k+\alpha q)(1-k-\alpha q)-\alpha^2q(1-q)
 =k\ell+\alpha(\beta-k)q>0.
\tag{CA.41}
$$

The affine expression is positive at zero and at $q_{\max}$, where it
is $\beta k(1+\alpha)/(1-\alpha)$. This proves uniqueness in (CA.35).
For fixed $Q_0$, $F(P_0,Q_0)$ decreases as $P_0$ increases above $Q_0$.
If $Q_0<q$, then $Q_0<q_{\max}$ and
$P_0\geq k+\alpha Q_0>Q_0$, so
$s\leq F(P_0,Q_0)\leq F(k+\alpha Q_0,Q_0)<s$, a contradiction.
Thus $Q_0\geq q$ and $P_0\geq p$, proving (CA.38).
For (CA.36), setting $\nu=\sqrt{q/(1-q)}$ in the angular equality yields

$$
(1-\beta-s^2)\nu^2-2s\sqrt{1-s^2}\nu+s^2-\ell=0.
\tag{CA.42}
$$

Rationalizing the root continuous from zero gives (CA.36). Its denominator
is positive since $\mathscr H_s\geq\beta k>0$, including a vanishing
leading coefficient in (CA.42). The two roots cannot collide before the
endpoint, and uniqueness on the angular interval selects this branch.
Direct endpoint substitution gives the stated $s=1$ values.

For an original height,
$\tfrac14\sum_{e,n}\rho_{y,e,n}=\tau_y:=\operatorname{diag}(p_y)\succ0$.
Thus the mean is the acquired cost at $\tau_y$. At this matrix all
$A,B,C$ are strictly positive. In the weak chain equality forces
$Q_0=Z_0=0$ and $P_0=k$; in the strong chain it forces
$P_0=p,Q_0=q,Z_0=0$. The actual aggregate diagonal is unchanged by
averaging. The positive supported slack from (CA.13) has trace zero
against $\tau_y\succ0$, so it is zero: conjugate by $\tau_y^{1/2}$ and
use that a positive trace-zero matrix vanishes. This proves exactly the
asserted equality prerequisites. Equality on an arbitrary rank-deficient
test does not supply this conclusion. $\square$

